{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And any manner of work, etc. For it is Yom Kippurim, etc.</b> The giving of this reason follows the path of what they said in Sotah, folio 40a — that verses are not recited while the priests bless the people, and the reason they stated in this language: Is there a servant who is being blessed and does not listen? End quote. And according to this approach He said, ״וְכָל מְלָאכָה לֹא תַעֲשׂוּ״, and the reason — ״כִּי יוֹם כִּפּוּרִים הוּא״, meaning that the sheer intensity of the day effects atonement. Now, can you find a person upon whom the time effects atonement while he is preoccupied with his work? And the one who reflects upon the intensity of the day — his heart will understand the One who effects atonement, for He is the Master who illuminates the day to prepare the preparation of this matter."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The citation from Sotah 40a concerns interrupting with scriptural verses during the priestly blessing; Or HaChaim applies its logic analogically to Yom Kippur. The final clause לְסַגֵּל סֶגֶל is rendered hyper-literally as 'to prepare the preparation'; the phrase is syntactically difficult and may carry a kabbalistic nuance."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:35.738343977Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}